A||||19||voyagerspe||th125 transmissions anyone every change ratio? (x-11 gears)||||1||
Z||000000||voyagerspe||12-28-2001||11:29 PM||||Ive read on this forum (in the archives)and on the yahoo x-11 club links that the chevy citation x-11 came with an optional 3.56 gear ratio.  I would love to get those gears into my th125.  Has anyone ever done this?  does any one have a source for the parts(the correct gears)? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Dave Gunsul||12-29-2001||12:16 AM||||The 85 and 86 V6 Fieros with the TH-125 already have a 3.65:1 final drive in them.<BR>The 87 and 88 V6 autos have a 3.33:1 final drive for improved gas mileage.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000002||voyagerspe||12-29-2001||01:24 AM||||Are you sure?? do you have the rpo # for the 3.65:1  tranny?  I believe in my 85 gt I have  3.06 gears.  I didnt know that 3.65 gears were used.  I thought that the 3.33:1 ratio was the lowest ratio used.  I really hope that you are right, any other info that you have would be great.  thanks<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000003||rockcrawl||12-29-2001||07:09 AM||||Gear ratios for automatic transaxles<BR>         1st     2nd    3rd<BR>MD9      2.84   1.60    1.00<P>       Final<BR>       Drive   Code   RPO<BR>1984   3.18    PF     MD9<BR>1985   3.06    CD     MD9<BR>1985   3.18    PF     MD9<BR>1986   3.06    6CD    MD9<BR>1986   3.18    6PF    MD9<BR>1987   3.33    7CPC   MD9<BR>1987   2.84    7PSC   MD9<BR>1988   3.33    8CPC   MD9<BR>1988   2.84    8PSC   MD9<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||voyagerspe||12-29-2001||09:11 AM||||thanks for the info rockcrawl, does anyone know if this 3.65:1 ratio exists in some other 125 transmission (other then a fiero)  Im on a quest to find a 3.56 125 tranny (or the gears)((i know they came in an x-11)) but if someone knows what cars a 3.65:1 gear was used ill try to find those gears or tranny instead.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-29-2001||02:25 PM||||Unless you are very good with TH125's I recomend you leave changing the finals to profesionals. I remember that you have to rip apart the whole tranny to change the finals. After that you've got to get the thing back together, and likely recalibrate the governor when your finished. It's nearly the same amount of work as a total tranny rebuild.<P>The biggest gears I've seen listed are 3.33 for TH125. There might be a deeper set for the AOD trans.... <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dave Gunsul||12-29-2001||03:33 PM||||Well according to Gary Witzenburgs book and <BR>several of Joe Wynmans articles the 85 and 86 V6 autos have a 365:1 ratio. I'm pretty sure i've got another source that says that too but i'll have to check. The ratios listed above are correct but they are for the 4 cyl autos. The 4cyl autos did have higher gears to make them more fuel efficient. <BR>From personal experience I can also tell you that there's about a 300 RPM difference between the 85-86 V6 autos and the 87-88 autos. When ever i've driven them the 87-88's are always at a lower RPM then the 85-86 autos are. This would point to a higher final drive in the 87-88 autos. I had a friend that had an 87 GT and I have compared his to my 86 GT and my 85 Se and they all had the same tire sizes on at that time and his 87 was always at a lower RPM then my two cars were. (all were autos BTW)<P>Here's a rundown of ALL the gear ratios both 4 and 6 cyl.<BR>84 sport coupe,SE: 3:18 (4cyl)<BR>85 fiero coupe,SE: 3.35 (4cyl)<BR>85 SE,GT: 3.65 (6cyl)<BR>86 coupe,sportcoupe,SE: 3.35 (4cyl)<BR>86 SE,GT: 3.65 (6cyl)<BR>87 coupe,sport coupe,SE: 2.84 (4cyl)<BR>87 SE,GT: 3.33<BR>88 coupe: 2.84<BR>88GT,Formula: 3.33<BR>Note: these are just the automatic final drive ratios not the manuals.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaun41178||12-29-2001||03:41 PM||||Ok you guys are confusing the 3.65 manual tranny with the auto tranny.  the lowest FD offered for a automatic Fiero was the 3.33.  In the 85 Gt autos had a 3.06.  I know some of the 4 cylinders in 84, their autos had 2.84's or something like that.  Witzenburg is wrong if it states the autos had 3.65 cause that is what the 4 spd muncie had.<P>The higher numercially the FD the higher the engine will be in the rpm band at a certain cruising speed.<P>My 85 Gt would do 70 mph at 3000 rpm.  Since the 87 and 88Gt have the 3.33 their rpm will be a tad higher at the same cruising speed but will help in the acceleration department.<P>All being equal a auto 85 Gt will have a slightly higher top end then a 87 or 88 GT<P>Good luck finding a auto tranny with that FD.  However perhaps GM still has some old stock laying around somewhere and you will have to purchase a new one.  Otherwise you are stuck to looking in j-yards and finding one could be a tough job considering the small amount of x-11's with that tranny.  Good luck however.<P>------------------<BR>SOLD 85 GT w/ nitrous.<BR>Looking for another.<BR>FOR SALE: Aluminum v6 block.<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dave Gunsul||12-29-2001||04:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>Ok you guys are confusing the 3.65 manual tranny with the auto tranny.  the lowest FD offered for a automatic Fiero was the 3.33.  In the 85 Gt autos had a 3.06.  I know some of the 4 cylinders in 84, their autos had 2.84's or something like that.  Witzenburg is wrong if it states the autos had 3.65 cause that is what the 4 spd muncie had.<P>The higher numercially the FD the higher the engine will be in the rpm band at a certain cruising speed.<P>My 85 Gt would do 70 mph at 3000 rpm.  Since the 87 and 88Gt have the 3.33 their rpm will be a tad higher at the same cruising speed but will help in the acceleration department.<P>All being equal a auto 85 Gt will have a slightly higher top end then a 87 or 88 GT<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>No i'm afraid you have it backward: the 85 and 86 V6 autos run at a higher RPM than the 87 and 88's do. I will say again: I have driven a couple and compared them DIRECTLY.<BR>all three cars had the same sixe tires on them and my 85 and 86 both ran at a higher RPM than my friends 87 did. I have also driven others and every one has supported this. Joe Wynnman also lists the 85 and 86 V6 autos as being 3.65:1 final drive. <BR>You also have to take into account that the Fiero basically got everything (and owes everything) from the X-11. I'm sure some of you won't be convinced but all I can say is go out and drive an 85-86 V6 auto with the same size tires as an 87-88 V6 auto and you'll see the difference for yourself!<BR>Remember though that the 4 cyl autos have a different ratio than the V6's do so if you use them as a guide then you won't come up with the same results.<BR>BTW yes the 4-speed sticks for 85-86 also had a 3.65:1 final drive BUT the gears in the trans, besides having 4 instead of 3, were different. Here's the 85-86 auto vs 4-speed manual ratios:<BR>85-86 auto trans ratios: 2.84 1st, 1.60 2cond, and 1.00 3rd with a 3.65:1 final drive<BR>85-86 4-speed manual gears: 3.31 1st, 1.95 2cond, 1.24 3rd, and .81 4th gear with a 3.65:1 final drive.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shaun41178||12-29-2001||06:50 PM||||Ok well somebody is wrong here.  I was basing my info that I got off the internet from another Fiero site.  I believe it was the PFKB or something like that.  It specifically states all the RPO codes for the th 125 tranny for all models including ones that did not come in a Fiero and it says the rpo code for the 85 Gt is CD and that it has a 3.06 tranny in it.<P>Yes I know Joe Wynman printed it up somewhere but that doesn't mean it is right.  I also know Focoa printed it up too but I am guessing they got it from Joe.  Don't you tink Joe just got his info from Witzenburg and that Witzenburg is wrong as Witzenburg got the manual and auto tranny miced up?  However my info could be wrong as well.  <P>If the 85 GT fiero tranny runs at a higher rpm at 70 mph then what does a 87Gt with the GX3 rpo code(3.33) run at at 70 mph?<P>If noone wants to check at 70 mph then maybe check at 65?  My 85 Gt ran at 2600 rpm at 65 mph.<P>i think Me and Rockcrawl have the same info cause in the info he gives he doesnt' list anything higher then a 3.33 for a Fiero.<P>Perhaps someone has to go directly to GM to find this answer out.<P>||||reg||3
Z||000010||voyagerspe||12-30-2001||01:15 AM||||thanks for you info and im sorry i didnt mean to start a fight  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Ive got a couple of calls into some Tranny sources to try to get rpo codes on 125 trannys.  Ive got a 125 teardown manual coming and maybe it will shed some light on what years came with what.  I do know that a Rpo of 3CD is 3.56 gears ( this info came from an X-11 website) but I can find no reference to 3.65 gears in a 125.  if we werent getting covered in snow right now id take out my 85gt & 87 gt and run them at 70 to check the rpms (but its going to have to wait till spring  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) ||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||12-30-2001||01:40 AM||||I owned an 83 X-11 with a 3CD automatic 125. When I went to find the final drive from the dealer (among other things), it wasn't listed in their books. I called Chevorlet and they said it wasn't listed there either. <P>I gave them the VIN and a few days later than came back with the built sheet and said it was 3.65 (or 3.56, can't remember. That was in 1986). That came from Chevy. True or not, beats me. I will say that it turned 3000 RPM at 60MPH with the TCC locked. Pretty damn low gears. It would redline in third gear at 121. Another 84 X-11 auto turned 2600 at 60 MPH. I think those were 3.33's. I don't recall the X-11 getting 3.06's. Could be though.<P>Strange car in other ways. It had the Z19 option (X-11 sport package), but had the long grandma speedo and no tach but had the wiring for the gauge package (which is different) than the grandma speedo wiring.<P>It had the optional swing out windows but they weren't on the build sheet.<P>The car was purchased by Arby's in Michigan, delivered to Florida with California emissions package. One of 50 cars made special for them (yeah, they forgot the gauges). They gave them to managers of the top selling resturants. I bought it from the original owner after they gave it to their 16 year old son and he held it to the floor with a beer between his legs and got caught.<P>There were other little strange things about it. Fun car either way. Interesting history.<P>I wouldn't spend any time looking for the those gears since I could never nail it down. That information was copied from my original X-11 site.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000012||voyagerspe||12-30-2001||01:50 AM||||"I wouldn't spend any time looking for the those gears since I could never nail it down. "<P>Ohhhhhhhh I want those gears  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>terry, do you have any insight into any other cars that would have the 3.56:1 or 3.65:1 gears? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||12-30-2001||02:23 AM||||My guess would be only cars (X body) with VIN Z engines (HO V6's). But as I said, it might be the legendary 89 Fiero......<P>That was a long time ago when I checked into all of that.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Dave Gunsul||12-30-2001||03:49 AM||||I only tested at 60 because that's what speed I usually drive at. My 85 SE (yes it's a V6) and my 86 GT both have autos and they both are doing about 25-2600 RPMs at 60<BR>my friends 87 GT did about 22-2300 RPMs at 60. I know there's a mathamatical way to figure this out but I don't know how you do that but the RPM difference is clearly lower in the 87 than the other two cars and always was when ever I drove his car. I drove it a lot when his license was suspended and I was looking for this difference specifically because I have heard this same argument before. As Sean already said, we really don't know which source is right and which is wrong but I have driven these three cars a lot and I have always noticed the difference in RPMs between them. I have also driven an 88 Formula with an auto before that I would swear was at the same 22-2300 RPM at 60 that his was but I only drove that once and it was a long time ago so I wouldn't stake my life on that memory.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>The other cars I have driven a lot though (one just tonight) and I do remember those very well. I suppose the final point is that<BR>there's only a 300 RPM difference between the two anyway and it wouldn't really make a difference off the line to be worth the effort anyway. <P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cozmo||12-30-2001||10:48 AM||||Is there any markings on the T125 that indicate the final drive ratios?<BR>Also, what about torque converter stall speeds?<BR>What types are out there that we can use?<P>BobC||||reg||5
Z||000016||voyagerspe||12-30-2001||12:04 PM||||Thanks for everyones input.  I got a atsg manual for the 125 coming and i hope it will help me find out if other gears a doable.  The reason that I want the lowest gears possible is that I race my v8 fiero at my local track (which is only an 1/8 mile) with my 3.33 gears the car is a rocket but if i can get even a better ratio i think i could do much much better.  This is not a car that get driven very often so fuel milage is not an issue, but in a race every 1/10th of a second counts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>cozmo you can id a 125 by the rpo# <BR>its a sticker on the side of the valvebody oil pan.  problem is they are usually missing.  then you have to id the vin number and get the # from there.<P>the info that i have (I dont know if its acurate) is :<P>fp3 fw2 fw9 f47 f90 = 3.06:1<P>f16 f17 f62 f75 = 2.84<P>f77 f79 gx3 = 3.33<P>3cd = 3.56<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||12-30-2001||04:56 PM||||Anyone check what the U-van uses? I have one and I wonder if they are lower than 3.33's. Maybe since it has to drag that beast around with a 3.1L V6.<P>Just a thought. ||||reg||5
Z||000018||Dave Gunsul||12-30-2001||11:19 PM||||I'm really starting to wonder what the autos came with now myself. I know from driving my friends 87 that it had lower RPMs then my two cars did but maybe his speedo was wrong and giving me false readings and Gary Wit. and Joe Wyn. are also wrong.<BR>Somebodies just got to find out for sure because if my sources are wrong then I would really like to know!<BR>I would think that with a V8 in a light car the last thing you would have to worry about would be more of a mechanical advantage off the line! With all that wonderful torque I would think that traction would be your main concern.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
